ated by the fixed before-prepare and after-executor boot-identity snapshots. Traffic-continuity checks during each backend's downtime show the load balancer correctly routing all requests to the surviving backend, demonstrating no loss of service during single-backend restarts.\",\"limitations\":[\"The distribution of the 8-request round-robin sample on node1 was skewed (6/2) rather than perfectly alternating; this does not invalidate the load-balancing requirement but is noted as an imperfect demonstration of even distribution.\",\"After each backend reboot, the app's reported 'backend' identifier switched from the expected node alias ('node2'/'node3') to the machine's raw hostname (e.g., 'vm-rhel7-2454928906'), suggesting the app relies on an environment variable not persisted/passed through OpenRC after restart; this is a cosmetic naming inconsistency in the verification evidence, not a functional failure, since health and inventory responses remained correct.\",\"The full content of the deployed app.py was transmitted as a base64-encoded blob and not shown in the captured evidence, so the application's internal implementation could not be independently reviewed beyond its observed HTTP behavior.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"Inventory web application running on node2, listening on port 8080","status":"satisfied","summary":"OpenRC service inventory-app was created, enabled, and started on node2 (cmd-80c91d7547874407); curl to node2:8080/health returned a healthy JSON response (cmd-9448095f230f417f). The after-executor lifecycle snapshot independently confirms python3 listening on 0.0.0.0:8080 on node2.","evidence_ids":["cmd-80c91d7547874407","cmd-9448095f230f417f","global:after_executor:node2:listening-sockets"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"Inventory web application running on node3, listening on port 8080","status":"satisfied","summary":"OpenRC service inventory-app was created, enabled, and started on node3 (cmd-2c1344b